\section{Estimators}{

\code{\link[=mnl]{mnl()}} fits the multinomial / conditional logit with case-specific
covariates (alternative-specific coefficients), alternative-specific
covariates (generic coefficients), alternative-specific constants, varying
choice sets, frequency weights, offsets, unit fixed effects (with the
split-panel jackknife \code{bias_correct = "jackknife"}), and analytic,
outer-product, robust, cluster-robust, or bootstrap standard errors.
\code{\link[=imnl]{imnl()}} fits the baseline-inflated multinomial logit of Bagozzi and
Marchetti (2017) for any inflated alternative, with a logit or probit split
equation, and with several inflated alternatives the parametrized logit
captivity model of Swait and Ben-Akiva (1987), which generalizes the dogit
of Gaudry and Dagenais (1979). \code{\link[=nlogit]{nlogit()}} fits the two-level nested logit, \code{\link[=hev]{hev()}} the
heteroskedastic extreme value model, \code{\link[=mnprobit]{mnprobit()}} the multinomial probit
with a free covariance, and \code{\link[=remnl]{remnl()}} the multinomial logit with random
alternative-specific intercepts per unit; these accept a wide layout (one row
per case) and a long layout (one row per case-alternative pair; see the \code{id}
and \code{alt} arguments). \code{\link[=rologit]{rologit()}} fits the rank-ordered (exploded) logit to
complete, partial, or tied rankings in the long layout.
}

\section{Separation}{

\code{\link[=separation]{separation()}} checks exactly, by a linear program, whether the data of an
\code{\link[=mnl]{mnl()}} or \code{\link[=rologit]{rologit()}} fit are separated (no finite maximum-likelihood
estimate). The maximum-likelihood likelihood-ratio tests then compare the
suprema of the log-likelihoods. \code{firth = TRUE} (Firth's penalty) and
\code{prior = coef_prior(...)} (normal, Cauchy or t priors) give finite
penalized estimates, with profile intervals and penalized likelihood-ratio
tests.
}

